Go-based malware like **Chaos** uses DNS tunneling to exfiltrate data via encoded TXT queries: ASCII characters are Base32-encoded and split across subdomains (e.g., `U2FsdGVkX1[...].example.com`). It evades detection by tunneling over **DNS over HTTPS (DoH)** using `cloudflare-dns.com` or `1.1.1.1`, bypassing traditional DNS logging. Static analysis uses `Ghidra` with Go decompiler plugins (e.g., **ghidra-go-plugin**) to recover logic; dynamic analysis uses `Unicorn Engine` to emulate Go runtime calls. The malware employs `T1071.004` (Application Layer Protocol: DNS) and `T1568.002` (DNS Staging). Network detection is built via a `Zeek` script:
```zeek
event dns_request(c: connection, msg: dns_msg, query: string, qtype: count, qclass: count) {
    if (|query| > 50 && /^[A-Z2-7]{32,}\./ in query) {
        NOTICE([$note=DNS_Tunneling_Detected, $msg=fmt("Possible DNS tunnel: %s", query)]);
    }
}
```
Mitigations include DNS sinkholing via `Pi-hole` with custom blocklists and enforcing DNSSEC. A real-world campaign (2022–2024) attributed to **Storm-0978** used similar DNS tunnels to evade EDRs in financial institutions.

